            0.138,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "140"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.782,
            0.104,
            0.899,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "MATHEMATICS"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.319,
            0.146,
            0.715,
            0.189
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{\\mathrm{PD}}{\\mathrm{BD}} = \\tan 30^{\\circ} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\quad  or  \\quad \\mathrm{BD} = \\mathrm{PD} \\sqrt{3}",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.199,
            0.351,
            0.217
          ],
          "content": "In right $\\Delta \\; \\mathrm { P A C }$ , we have"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.318,
            0.231,
            0.51,
            0.272
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{PC}{AC} = \\tan 45^{\\circ} = 1",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.283,
            0.142,
            0.299
          ],
          "content": "i.e.,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.307,
            0.155,
            0.325
          ],
          "content": "Also,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.326,
            0.281,
            0.772,
            0.326
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned} & PC  =  AC  \\\\& PC  =  PD  +  DC ,  therefore ,  PD  +  DC  =  AC .\\\\ \\end{aligned}",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.338,
            0.873,
            0.359
          ],
          "content": "Since, $\\mathrm { A C } = \\mathrm { B D }$ and $\\mathrm{DC} = \\mathrm{AB} = 8$ m, we get $PD + 8 = BD = PD\\sqrt{3} (\\text{Why?})$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.398,
            0.206,
            0.418
          ],
          "content": "This gives"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.326,
            0.375,
            0.829,
            0.436
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{PD} = \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{3} - 1} = \\frac{8\\left( \\sqrt{3} + 1 \\right)}{\\left( \\sqrt{3} + 1 \\right)\\left( \\sqrt{3} - 1 \\right)} = 4\\left( \\sqrt{3} + 1 \\right)\\mathrm{m}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.443,
            0.895,
            0.504
          ],
          "content": "$\\mathrm { S o } ,$ the height of the multi-storeyed building is $\\left\\{ 4\\left( \\sqrt{3} + 1 \\right) + 8 \\right\\} = 4\\left( 3 + \\sqrt{3} \\right)$ m and the distance between the two buildings is also $4(3 + \\sqrt{3})  m$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.517,
            0.902,
            0.575
          ],
          "content": "**Example 7 :** From a point on a bridge across a river, the angles of depression of the banks on opposite sides of the river are $3 0 ^ { \\circ }$ and $4 5 ^ { \\circ }$ , respectively. If the bridge is at a height of 3 m from the banks, find the width of the river."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.58,
            0.422,
            0.754
          ],
          "content": "**Solution :** In Fig 9.10, A and B represent points on the bank on opposite sides of the river, so that AB is the width of the river. P is a point on the bridge at a height of 3 m , i . e . , $\\mathrm { D P } = 3 \\mathrm { ~ m } .$ We are interested to determine the width of the river, which is the length of the side AB of the D APB."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.438,
            0.588,
            0.894,
            0.676
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.623,
                0.693,
                0.722,
                0.712
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 9.10"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.762,
            0.157,
            0.779
          ],
          "content": "Now,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.255,
            0.761,
            0.423,
            0.778
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{AB} = \\mathrm{AD} + \\mathrm{DB}",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_17.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.786,
            0.372,
            0.805
          ],
          "content": "In right $\\Delta  APD , \\angle  A = 30^{\\circ}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.832,
            0.136,
            0.849
          ],
          "content": "So,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.217,
            0.818,
            0.369,
            0.859
          ],
          "content": "\\tan   30^{\\circ}   =   \\frac{ PD }{ AD }",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_20.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
